% A multi-model abduction example, richer than grass_is_wet / sunglasses.
%
% "the loan is approved" can hold in several ways: creditworthiness has two
% independent routes (a good credit score OR a guarantor) and so does collateral
% (owning property OR a large deposit). Each base condition is OPEN (abducible),
% so s(CASP) ASSUMES a minimal set to justify approval — yielding one stable
% model ("possible world") per combination, each with its own assumption set.
% The guard "it is not the case that the applicant is bankrupt" is a constructive
% negation: in the s(CASP) justification it is a *proved* sub-tree, not a bare
% absence. The `bankrupt` scenario closes every world (the loan is refused).

the target language is: scasp.

the predicates are:
    the loan is approved.
    the applicant is creditworthy.
    the applicant has collateral.
    the applicant has a good credit score; assumable.
    the applicant has a guarantor; assumable.
    the applicant owns property; assumable.
    the applicant has a large deposit; assumable.
    the applicant is bankrupt.

the knowledge base loan includes:

the loan is approved if
    the applicant is creditworthy
    and the applicant has collateral
    and it is not the case that the applicant is bankrupt.

the applicant is creditworthy if
    the applicant has a good credit score.

the applicant is creditworthy if
    the applicant has a guarantor.

the applicant has collateral if
    the applicant owns property.

the applicant has collateral if
    the applicant has a large deposit.

scenario application is:
    approval expects answers [
        "the loan is approved",
        "the loan is approved",
        "the loan is approved",
        "the loan is approved"
    ] and unknowns [
        "the applicant has a good credit score",
        "the applicant has a guarantor",
        "the applicant has a large deposit",
        "the applicant owns property"
    ].

scenario bankrupt is:
    the applicant is bankrupt.

query approval is:
    the loan is approved.
